Explain canary vs blue-green tradeoffs:-
 
Canary and blue-green deployments are deployment strategies that minimize risk during software releases by controlling how new versions reach users.

Blue-green switches all traffic instantly between two full environments, while canary gradually routes a small portion of traffic to the new version first.[2][5]

Key Tradeoffs
Blue-green offers zero-downtime deploys and instant rollback by flipping a router switch, but requires double the infrastructure and exposes all users at once if issues slip through. Canary reduces blast radius by testing with subsets of users and saves resources by sharing infrastructure, though it demands sophisticated traffic splitting, monitoring, and slower rollouts.[3][5][2]

## Comparison Table

| Aspect              | Blue-Green                          | Canary                              |
|---------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| Rollout Speed      | Instant full switch [5]       | Gradual over time [5]         |
| Resource Use       | High (two full envs) [3]      | Lower (shared infra) [3]      |
| Risk Exposure      | All users at switch [2]       | Limited initial users [2]     |
| Rollback Ease      | Immediate switch back [5]     | Gradual traffic reduction [5] |
| Complexity         | Simpler setup [3]             | Needs monitoring/routing [3]  |

Choose blue-green for critical apps needing fast recovery; opt for canary when budget limits resources or real-user feedback guides iterations.[2]

