Great question. This is core networking + cloud security knowledge, and interviewers expect a clear mental model, not just definitions.

I’ll explain TLS from basics, then how ALB terminates TLS, with diagrams, flow, and real-world implications.

---

 1️⃣ What is TLS?

TLS (Transport Layer Security) is a cryptographic protocol that secures data in transit between a client and a server.

 TLS guarantees

1. 🔐 Confidentiality – data is encrypted
2. 🛡 Integrity – data cannot be altered
3. 🆔 Authentication – server identity is verified

👉 TLS is the modern version of SSL
👉 HTTPS = HTTP + TLS

---

 2️⃣ What Problem Does TLS Solve?

Without TLS:


Client ----(plain text)----> Server


❌ Anyone can read passwords, tokens, data

With TLS:


Client ----(encrypted)----> Server


✔ Data is unreadable to attackers

---

 3️⃣ How TLS Works (High Level)

 TLS Handshake (Simplified)


Client → Server: Hello (supported ciphers)
Server → Client: Certificate + Public Key
Client → Server: Encrypted secret
Both sides: Derive session key
Secure communication starts


After handshake:

 All data encrypted using symmetric key
 Fast & secure

---

 4️⃣ Where TLS Lives in Real Architecture


Browser
   |
TLS
   |
Load Balancer
   |
HTTP
   |
Spring Boot App


---

 5️⃣ What Does “TLS Termination” Mean?

TLS termination means:

> The TLS connection ends at the load balancer, not at your application.

 Before termination


Client ⇄ (TLS) ⇄ App


 After termination


Client ⇄ (TLS) ⇄ ALB ⇄ (HTTP) ⇄ App


✔ ALB decrypts traffic
✔ App receives plain HTTP

---

 6️⃣ How ALB Terminates TLS (Step-by-Step)

 Components involved

 ALB
 ACM (AWS Certificate Manager)
 Client (Browser / Mobile App)

---

 Step 1: Certificate Setup

 TLS certificate issued via ACM
 Domain: api.myapp.com
 Attached to ALB HTTPS listener (443)

---

 Step 2: Client Connects


https://api.myapp.com


 Browser initiates TLS handshake with ALB
 ALB presents certificate

---

 Step 3: Certificate Verification

Client checks:

 Certificate chain
 Domain match
 Expiry
 Trusted CA

✔ Verified → secure connection established

---

 Step 4: Data Decryption at ALB

 ALB decrypts incoming HTTPS traffic
 Forwards request as HTTP to backend


ALB → ECS / EC2 / EKS


---

 7️⃣ Why TLS Termination at ALB is Preferred

 🔥 Performance

 TLS handshake is CPU intensive
 ALB is optimized hardware
 Apps stay lightweight

---

 🔐 Security

 Certificates managed centrally
 Auto-renewal via ACM
 No private keys inside containers

---

 🚀 Scalability

 App instances scale independently
 No per-instance cert management

---

 🔄 Zero Downtime Deployments

 TLS unaffected during blue-green switch
 Cert remains on ALB

---

 8️⃣ Is Backend Traffic Secure After Termination?

By default:


ALB → App = HTTP (inside VPC)


 Is this safe?

✔ Yes (private VPC network)

 But for higher security:

 Enable HTTPS between ALB and services
 Mutual TLS (mTLS)


Client ⇄ TLS ⇄ ALB ⇄ TLS ⇄ App


---

 9️⃣ Comparison: TLS at App vs TLS at ALB

| Aspect          | TLS at ALB  | TLS at App   |
| --------------- | ----------- | ------------ |
| Cert management | Centralized | Per service  |
| CPU overhead    | ALB         | App          |
| Scaling         | Easy        | Hard         |
| mTLS            | Limited     | Full control |
| Zero downtime   | Easy        | Complex      |

👉 Most production systems terminate TLS at ALB

---

 🔟 Common Interview Traps (Answer Clearly)

 ❓ Is data unencrypted after ALB?

✔ Yes, unless re-encrypted
✔ Safe inside private network

 ❓ Does ALB support TLS 1.3?

✔ Yes (latest AWS ALBs)

 ❓ Can ALB do mTLS?

✔ Client certificate validation supported

---

 1️⃣1️⃣ One-Line Interview Answer

“TLS secures data in transit by encrypting communication. In AWS, ALB terminates TLS by handling the handshake using certificates from ACM, decrypting incoming HTTPS traffic, and forwarding it as HTTP to backend services, improving performance, security, and scalability.”

